Recursion is a function calling itself on a smaller version of the same problem.

Every recursive function needs two parts: a base case that stops the recursion, and a recursive case that moves toward the base case.

```python
def factorial(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return 1
    return n * factorial(n - 1)
```

Without the base case you get infinite recursion and a stack overflow. Each call sits on the call stack until it returns, so deep recursion costs memory.
